      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\n**Document-level:**\n- Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01, revision 9\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 2563125\n- Page total: 13\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 22 September 2023\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V. (OIML Issuing Authority NL1)\n- Address: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Title: Certification Board? Actually \"Certification Board\" appears near the name. The layout: \"Issuing Authority NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1 / 22 September 2023 / Certification Board\". Hmm, the person responsible is M.Ph.D. Schmidt. Title might be \"Certification Board\" — I'll include it as person_title.\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\n**Applicants and manufacturers:**\n- ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom (both applicant and manufacturer)\n\n**Manufacturers (production locations, page 13):**\n- ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom\n- ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd., No. 4528, Kangxin Highway, Pudong New District, Shanghai, 201319, P.R. China\n- ABB Inc., 125 E. County Line Road, Warminster, PA 18974, United States of America\n- ABB India Limited, Plot No. 5 & 6, 2nd Phase, Peenya Industrial Area, Bengaluru – 560058, Karnataka India\n\n**Certified type:**\n- Category: \"Water meter\" (or \"Measuring system\"? R49 water meter → category \"Water meter\")\n- Type designations: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4* — with wildcards: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z, and FET4* variants. Let me record: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4*\". The variants: X=1 standard, 3 advanced, 5 premium (mobile Comms MC); Y = 1, 2, 8, 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only); Z = R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore), F (full bore).\n\nSo type designations: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4XY.Z\"? Actually the text: \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\" — with the asterisk footnote describing X, Y, Z. So FET4 also has X, Y, Z presumably (FEW = flow sensor wall? FET = ?). FET4 is likely the transmitter-only version (sensor only). Let me record type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4XY.Z\"]. Hmm, better keep as stated: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\" and \"FET4*\". I'll use [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4XY.Z\"]? Risky to fabricate. Let me keep exactly: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\". Actually the markdown says \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4<sup>*</sup>\" — so both types share the X/Y/Z scheme per the footnote. I'll list: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\" and \"AquaMaster4 FET4XY.Z\"? The footnote applies to both: \"With X being... Y being... Z being...\" — the asterisk is on FET4 but the X,Y,Z applies to the whole pattern. To be safe: type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4XY.Z\"]. Hmm. Actually I think FET4 is the transmitter-only model, and its naming includes X, Y, Z too (FET4XY.Z). But since the cert text literally says \"FET4*\", I'll write \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\". Let me include both as they appear: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\" and \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\".\n\n- Module designation: not applicable → for water meters, maybe \"Measuring system\"? Components: flow sensor (FEW) and calculating/indicating device (transmitter). The cert mentions \"flow sensor and flow transmitter\". I could record components: measuring sensor (FEW4...) and calculating and indicating device (FET4/transmitter). The revision 9 says \"Addition of AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z Mobile comms variant of calculating and indicating device.\" So FET4 is likely the calculating and indicating device (transmitter). Hmm, actually FET = flow... transmitter? ABB AquaMaster: FET400 is the transmitter. FEW is the flow tube. So components: flow sensor FEW4XY.Z, and calculating and indicating device FET4*.\n\n- Description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\". The footnote about X, Y, Z can go into description or footnotes.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n- id: \"R49\" (canonical), edition: 1? R 49-1 (2013). The recommendation is \"R 49-1 (2013)\" — that's part 1 of R49, edition 2013. Canonical id: \"R49\", edition: 2013? The schema says edition (int). Hmm — for OIML recommendations, edition is like \"2013\". I'll put edition: 2013, and maybe note part 1. There's no \"part\" field, so maybe id: \"R49-1\"? The rule says canonical e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". For R49-1, canonical could be \"R49-1\" or \"R49\". I'll use \"R49-1\" to preserve part info? The example says \"R60\" for \"OIML R 60\". R 49-1 is a specific part. I think \"R49-1\" is acceptable canonical. Hmm, but title: \"Water meters intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water\". I'll use id \"R49-1\", edition 2013.\n\nActually let me think: schema: recommendation: id (canonical, e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\"), edition (int). For R 49-1 (2013), I'd do id: \"R49-1\", edition: 2013, scheme: \"A\", accuracy_classes: [1, 2].\n\n**Test reports:**\n- NMi-2254799-01, 14 August 2020, 235 pages, type_evaluation_report\n- NMi-2254799-03, 30 September 2020, 44 pages\n- NMi-2254799-04, 24 December 2020, 180 pages\n- NMi-2564429-01, 02 August 2021, 26 pages\n- NMi-2496280-01, 26 August 2021, 29 pages\n- NMi-2496280-02, 23 November 2021, 25 pages\n- NMi-2563125-01, 22 September 2023, 94 pages\n\nRole: they say \"the associated reports\" — these are OIML Type Evaluation Reports. I'll use \"type_evaluation_report\" for all.\n\n**Documentation:** Documentation folder no. T11851-7 → test_reports entry with role \"documentation_file\"? The schema role includes documentation_file. Yes: id \"T11851-7\", role \"documentation_file\". No date. Include pages: null (omit).\n\n**Revision history:**\n- Initial, 14 August 2020, First issue.\n- 1, 30 September 2020, Additional evaluation of reduced bore larger sizes up to DN600.\n- 2, 24 December 2020, Additional evaluation of (virtual) full bore sensor and additional software version\n- 3, 02 August 2021, Additional evaluation for extending temperature to –25 °C and for E2 class and O class for the flow transmitter. Addition of software version.\n- 4, 26 August 2021, Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1200.\n- 5, 23 November 2021, Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1800.\n- 6, 20 May 2022, Addition of accuracy class 1 to DN1800.\n- 7, 30 August 2022, Software version updates due to addition of microprocessors.\n- 8, 3 July 2023, Software update\n- 9, 22 September 2023, Addition of AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z Mobile comms variant of calculating and indicating device. Software update\n\nRevision as string: \"0\" for Initial? The schema says revision string e.g. \"0\". \"Initial\" → \"0\"? Hmm, better keep \"Initial\"? The example says revision (string, e.g. \"0\" not 0). I'll use \"0\" for initial? That might be misrepresenting. The table says \"Initial\". I'll use \"Initial\" as string — it's a string. Or \"0\"? Rule 10 in examples: revision (string, e.g. \"0\" not 0). It's about type not content. I'll use \"Initial\" to be faithful.\n\n**Footnotes:**\n- The asterisk footnote: \"* With X being 1 for standard or 3 for advanced or 5 for premium (mobile Comms - MC) variant; Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only); Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\"\n- Footnote [1] on Table 4: \"The software version and checksum are displayed on the meter during the circular sequence every 10 minutes. The software version and checksum are displayed sequentially with first for the Measurement part then the Communications part.\"\n\n**Characteristics type_level (from Tables 1 & 2):**\n\nCommon: measuring principle: Electromagnetic; accuracy class 1 and 2; environmental class O (installed outdoors); electromagnetic environment E2 (Tables 1) but Table 2 (FEW45) has E1 for transmitter with external antenna cable, E2 without; mechanical class M1; temperature range ambient -25/+55 °C; water temperature class T50 (+0.1 °C / +50 °C); MAP 1.6 MPa (16 bar); orientation all positions; flow profile sensitivity U0 and D0 except DN250+: U3 and D0; reverse flow: intended to measure reverse flow; pressure loss class varies by bore/size; power supply variants; software ID; checking facilities Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility.\n\nI'll encode type_level attributes:\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n- environmental_class: \"O\"\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\" with range +0.1/+50 °C. Could encode as water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit °C, and water_temperature_class: \"T50\".\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6, unit MPa (also 16 bar — maybe second entry or footnote). I'll do value 1.6, unit MPa.\n- reverse_flow: \"intended to measure reverse flow\" → reverse_flow_measured: true? I'll put value \"reverse flow measured\" — or \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\". Keep as string.\n- checking_facility: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"\n\nConfig-level: electromagnetic environment by model/config:\n- FEW41/FEW43: E2\n- FEW45: E1 with external antenna cable; E2 without\n\nPressure loss class by config (bore × size):\n- Reduced bore: DN40, DN50: Δp40 (0.40 bar); >DN50: Δp63 (0.63 bar)\n- (Virtual) Full bore: DN40–DN50: Δp10 (0.10 bar); DN65–DN200: Δp16 (0.16 bar); DN250+: Δp10 (0.10 bar)\n\nFlow profile sensitivity class by size: U0/D0 except full bore DN250+: U3/D0.\n\nPower supply per model:\n- FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z: AC mains 95–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz; renewable DC 6–32 V; replaceable battery 2× 3.6 V (range 2.9–3.6 V)\n- FEW45Y.Z: AC mains 95–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz incl. 4× 3.6 V batteries; renewable 6–32 V incl 4× 3.6 V; battery 6× 3.6 V (range 2.9–3.6 V)\n\nI could encode power supply in model_level with sub-attributes: mains_voltage (95–240 V), mains_frequency (50–60 Hz), dc_voltage (6–32 V), battery_count, battery_voltage (3.6 V), battery_operating_range (2.9–3.6 V).\n\n**Model family:**\nThe certificate covers a family: models vary by:\n- transmitter/sensor variant: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z (X=1,3,5)\n- bore: reduced (Z=R), virtual full bore (V), full bore (F)\n- power: AC mains vs battery/renewable\n- meter size DN40...DN1800\n- accuracy class 1, 2\n\nThe big tables 5–8 are flow rate matrices by meter size × accuracy class × power × bore. And tables 9–12 indicating device characteristics by size × bore × power.\n\nThis is a huge matrix. Best approach:\n- matrix_tables: create one entry per Table 5–8 (flow rates) and Tables 9–12 (indicating device).\n- model_family.models: one per DN size? There are ~40 sizes × 2 classes × 2 power × 2-3 bore... too many. The R137 example created models per size. Here, I could create models per DN size (e.g., \"DN40\", \"DN50\", ...), with attributes varying in model_level: q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio. But q1/q2/q3/q4 depend on size AND class AND power AND bore. That's a 4-dimensional matrix.\n\nApproach: use matrix_tables for the full tables (4 flow tables + 4 indicating tables), and model_level for characteristics that vary by size within the default axes. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, treat models as combos: model_id like \"DN40 reduced bore AC mains class 1\"? That's ~170 entries. Too much, but maybe I can be smarter: model_id per meter size (DN40..DN1800) and put config_level axes: bore (reduced/full/virtual full), power (AC mains/battery), accuracy class (1/2). The flow values then go into matrix_tables fully.\n\nGiven the effort, I'll do:\n- model_family: family_name \"AquaMaster4\", models = one per DN size appearing across tables (DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600, DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800). That's 29 sizes.\n- model_level: for each of q1/q2/q3/q4? They vary by class, power, bore — too many combos for model_level values (each model would need many values). Better: keep the full tables in matrix_tables and populate model_level only with attributes that vary by size with unique values... but q3 varies per row.\n\nHmm. Let me think about what's practical and useful. The matrix_tables preserve the full data. model_level could include a few size-dependent attributes where a single value per size works, e.g., nothing really. Actually maybe I should do model_level for q3 by (model=DN size) with values conditioned? The values[] entries are {model, value}. If q3 is same across power/bore/class for a given size (check: DN40: AC reduced 25, battery reduced 25, AC full 40, battery full 40 → differs). So no.\n\nI think the cleanest: matrix_tables with the 4 flow tables (name includes bore+power) each with rows keyed by size+class, and 4 indicating-device tables. Then model_level: I could add \"indicating_range_max\", \"indicating_range_min\", \"verification_scale_interval_max\" — but these also vary by bore/power. They're in the matrix tables.\n\nAlternatively, model_level with attribute entries per table row... no.\n\nI'll populate model_family.models per DN size, and include matrix_tables for all 8 tables (5–12). For model_level, maybe include q3 (permanent flow rate) per size per config as config_level entries? Actually the schema has config_level: attribute × config-axis matrix with values {condition, value}. I could encode:\n\nconfig_level:\n- attribute \"electromagnetic_environment\", axis \"transmitter antenna configuration\": [{condition \"FEW45 with external antenna cable\", value \"E1\"}, {condition \"FEW45 without external antenna cable\", value \"E2\"}, {condition \"FEW41/FEW43\", value \"E2\"}]\n- attribute \"pressure_loss_class\", axis \"bore and meter size\": entries...\n- attribute \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\", axis \"bore and meter size\": U0/D0 for sizes < DN250, U3/D0 for full bore DN250+\n- attribute \"power supply\", axis \"variant\": ...\n\nThat fits config_level nicely.\n\nFor the flow matrices, matrix_tables is the right place. Let me build them.\n\n**Table 5: Reduced bore – AC mains powered** rows (meter_size, accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio):\n- DN40, 1: 0.05, 0.08, 25, 31.25, 500\n- DN40, 2: 0.025, 0.04, 25, 31.25, 1000\n- DN50, 1: 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\n- DN50, 2: 0.04, 0.064, 40, 50, 1000\n- DN65, 1&2: 0.063, 0.1008, 63, 78.75, 1000\n- DN80, 1&2: 0.1, 0.16, 100, 125, 1000\n- DN100, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN100, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN125, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN125, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN150, 1&2: 0.4, 0.64, 400, 500, 1000\n- DN200, 1&2: 0.63, 1.008, 630, 787.5, 1000\n- DN250, 1: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\n- DN250, 2: 1, 1.6, 1000, 1250, 1000\n- DN300, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN300, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n- DN350, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN350, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n- DN400, 1&2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n- DN450, 1&2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n- DN500, 1: 8, 12.8, 4000, 5000, 500\n- DN500, 2: 4, 6.4, 4000, 5000, 1000\n- DN600, 1: 12.6, 20.16, 6300, 7875, 500\n- DN600, 2: 6.3, 10.08, 6300, 7875, 1000 (from page 6 table continuation)\n\n**Table 6: Reduced bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered:**\n- DN40, 1&2: 0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.25, 400\n- DN50, 1&2: 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\n- DN65, 1: 0.39375, 0.63, 63, 78.75, 160\n- DN65, 2: 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400\n- DN80, 1: 0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160\n- DN80, 2: 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n- DN100, 1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n- DN100, 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n- DN125, 1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n- DN125, 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n- DN150, 1&2: 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400\n- DN200, 1&2: 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400\n- DN250, 1: 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\n- DN250, 2: 2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400\n- DN300, 1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n- DN300, 2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n- DN350, 1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n-