0, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1500, 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1500, 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80 (continuation page 9)\n- DN1600, 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1600, 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1650, 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1650, 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1800, 1&2: 317.5, 507.9, 16000, 20000, 50\n\n**Table 8: Full bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered:**\n- DN40, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 40, 50, 125\n- DN40, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 40, 50, 250\n- DN50, 1: 0.504, 0.8064, 63, 78.75, 125\n- DN50, 2: 0.252, 0.4032, 63, 78.75, 250\n- DN65, 1&2: 0.4, 0.64, 100, 125, 250\n- DN80, 1&2: 0.64, 1.024, 160, 200, 250\n- DN100, 1&2: 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n- DN125, 1&2: 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n- DN150, 1: 5.04, 8.064, 630, 787.5, 125\n- DN150, 2: 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\n- DN200, 1: 8, 12.8, 1000, 1250, 125\n- DN200, 2: 4, 6.4, 1000, 1250, 250\n- DN250, 1: 12.8, 20.48, 1600, 2000, 125\n- DN250, 2: 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250 (continuation page 10)\n- DN300, 1: 20, 32, 2500, 3125, 125\n- DN300, 2: 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\n- DN350, 1&2: 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250\n- DN400, 1&2: 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250\n- DN450, 1&2: 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n- DN500, 1&2: 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n- DN600, 1: 78.75, 126, 6300, 7875, 80\n- DN600, 2: 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\n- DN700, 1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n- DN700, 2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN750, 1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n- DN750, 2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN800, 1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n- DN800, 2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN900, 2: 158.7, 254, 10000, 12500, 63\n- DN1000, 2: 160, 250, 16000, 20000, 100\n- DN1050, 2: 160, 250, 16000, 20000, 100\n- DN1100, 2: 254, 406.3, 16000, 20000, 63\n- DN1200, 2: 254, 406.3, 16000, 20000, 63\n- DN1350, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1400, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1500, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1600, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1650, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n\n**Indicating device tables (9–12):**\n\nTable 9 (Reduced bore, AC mains): columns meter_size, indicating_range_max_m3, indicating_range_min_m3, verification_scale_interval_max_m3:\n- DN40, DN50, DN65: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN80: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n- DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN250: 9999999, 9999999, 0.001\n- DN300–DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n\nTable 10 (Reduced bore, battery/renewable):\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN200: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n- DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n\nTable 11 (Full bore, AC mains) spans pages 11-12:\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN200, DN250, DN300: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1 (page 12 continuation)\n- DN700–DN1200: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\n- DN1350–DN1800: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nTable 12 (Full bore, battery/renewable):\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n- DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n- DN200–DN500: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN600: 99999999, 9999999, 0.1\n- DN700, DN750, DN800: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\n- DN900–DN1650: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nI could combine tables 9-12 into per-config rows where meter_size is a list. For the rows, I'll keep meter_size as a string like \"DN40, DN50, DN65\" as in source? Or expand? The schema says rows[] object per row. I'll keep the grouped sizes as string to be faithful and compact. Actually expanding would be very long. Keep grouped.\n\nHmm, but should I include the indicating tables in matrix_tables? The schema says matrix_tables preserves multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere. The flow tables also fit there. I'll include all 8 tables as matrix_tables entries. That's a lot of JSON but fine.\n\nWait — I should consider whether to populate model_level at all given the size. The instructions strongly emphasize matrix detection. The flow tables are matrix_tables; model_level for size-dependent attributes doesn't have unique values per model. But I can create model_level entries for q1..q4 and ratio using model ids that include config: e.g., models like \"DN40-class1-AC-reduced\"? That would explode. The R137 example shows model_level mirroring matrix_tables data. But with 4 config dimensions, model_id would need to encode all. I'll define models as DN sizes and rely on matrix_tables for flow values; add model_level for a couple of attributes that vary purely by model... e.g., nothing clean.\n\nAlternative: define model_id per variant family: \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\" (the three transmitter variants), and model_level for power supply attributes:\n- mains_voltage: FEW41/43/45: 95–240 V\n- mains_frequency: 50–60 Hz\n- dc_supply_voltage: 6–32 V\n- battery_count: 2 (FEW41/43), 6 (FEW45)\n- battery_voltage: 3.6 V\n- battery_operating_range: 2.9–3.6 V\n- battery_configuration: FEW45: 4× 3.6 V batteries included with AC mains and DC powered.\n\nThat works well: models = FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z. And meter sizes as config axis? Hmm, but model_family should probably reflect the actual instrument family. The instrument family is AquaMaster4 with models FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z (and FET4). The DN sizes are config axes.\n\nLet me restructure:\n- model_family.family_name: \"AquaMaster4\"\n- models: \n  - {model_id: \"FEW41Y.Z\", label: \"Standard variant (X=1)\", attributes: {bore_options: \"Z = R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore)\", ...}}\n  - {model_id: \"FEW43Y.Z\", label: \"Advanced variant (X=3)\"}\n  - {model_id: \"FEW45Y.Z\", label: \"Premium / mobile comms variant (X=5)\"}\n  - {model_id: \"FET4*\", label: \"Calculating and indicating device (transmitter)\"}? Hmm FET4 is the transmitter-only. Actually wait: \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\" — FEW is the complete meter (flow sensor + transmitter?) Hmm. Actually ABB: AquaMaster3 FET400... Let me think. ABB AquaMaster4: FET400 is flow transmitter; FEW400 is electromagnetic flow sensor; complete meters are \"AquaMaster4 FEW400/FET400\". Actually the combination: water meter = flow sensor FEW + transmitter FET. But the cert says type \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\". So the certified type comprises both. Y being \"1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only)\" — so integral version has sensor+transmitter as FEW; sensor-only also possible.\n\nSo components:\n- flow sensor: FEW4XY.Z (FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z)\n- calculating and indicating device (transmitter): FET4* (FET4XY.Z)\n\nGiven revision 9: \"Addition of AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z Mobile comms variant of calculating and indicating device\" — the calculating and indicating device variants correspond to X.\n\nOK here's my plan for model_family:\nmodels (one per certified variant):\n1. model_id \"FEW41Y.Z\", label \"Standard\", attributes {x_code: \"1\", ...}\n2. model_id \"FEW43Y.Z\", label \"Advanced\", attributes {x_code: \"3\"}\n3. model_id \"FEW45Y.Z\", label \"Premium (mobile comms, MC)\", attributes {x_code: \"5\"}\n\nAttributes include bore: Z ∈ {R, V, F}; version Y ∈ {1,2,8,9}.\n\nmodel_level characteristics (attribute × model):\n- ac_mains_voltage: FEW41/43/45 all {min 95, max 240} V\n- ac_mains_frequency: 50–60 Hz all\n- dc_supply_voltage: 6–32 V all\n- battery_cell_count: 2 vs 6\n- battery_cell_voltage: 3.6 V\n- battery_operating_voltage: 2.9–3.6 V\n- batteries_with_external_power: 4× 3.6V for FEW45 (vs none listed for 41/43)\n\nHmm, battery: FEW41/43: \"Replaceable Battery: 2x 3,6 V (operating range: 2,9 - 3,6 V)\". FEW45: \"Replaceable Battery: 6x 3,6 V (operating range: 2,9 - 3,6 V)\". Also FEW45's AC mains and DC supply include 4× 3.6 V replaceable batteries.\n\nmodel_level entries:\n- replaceable_battery_cell_count: {FEW41Y.Z: 2, FEW43Y.Z: 2, FEW45Y.Z: 6}\n- replaceable_battery_cell_voltage: 3.6 V (all)\n- replaceable_battery_operating_range: 2.9–3.6 V (all)\n- backup_battery_cell_count_with_external_power: {FEW45Y.Z: 4} only.\n\nAnd type_level:\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n- environmental_class: \"O\" (installed outdoors)\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- electromagnetic_environment: E2 — but varies (FEW45: E1/E2 by antenna config) → put in config_level.\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min: -25, max: 55} °C\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\"; water_temperature_range {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa (also 16 bar)\n- orientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\"\n- reverse_flow: \"sensor intended to measure reverse flow\" → true\n- accuracy_class: [1, 2] (top-level recommendation.accuracy_classes)\n- checking_facility: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: config by size/bore.\n\nconfig_level entries:\n1. attribute: electromagnetic_environment, axis: \"transmitter variant / antenna cable\", values: [{condition: \"FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z\", value \"E2\"}, {condition: \"FEW45Y.Z with external antenna cable\", value \"E1\"}, {condition: \"FEW45Y.Z without external antenna cable\", value \"E2\"}]. Note value as string with unit null.\n2. attribute: flow_profile_sensitivity_class, axis: \"meter size (full bore)\", values: [{condition: \"DN sizes smaller than DN250\", value \"U0 and D0 (0 × DN upstream, 0 × DN downstream)\"}, {condition: \"full bore DN250 and larger\", value \"U3 and D0 (3 × DN upstream, 0 × DN downstream)\"}]\n3. attribute: pressure_loss_class, axis: \"bore and meter size\", values: [{condition \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\", value 0.40 unit bar}, {condition \"reduced bore, larger than DN50\", 0.63 bar}, {condition \"virtual or full bore, DN40 and DN50\", 0.10 bar}, {condition \"virtual or full bore, DN65 to DN200\", 0.16 bar}, {condition \"virtual or full bore, DN250 and larger\", 0.10 bar}]. Include pressure loss class label too (Δp40 etc.)? I'll include class in a separate field maybe \"Δp class\" combined: value 0.40 bar and condition includes \"Δp 40\". I can put value object plus a second attribute \"pressure_loss_class_designation\". Simpler: keep value as number with unit bar, condition includes class.\n\n4. attribute: power_supply, axis \"power source\": condition \"AC mains\", value {min 95 max 240} V; frequency separate.\n\nAlso Q3/Q1 note: \"flow rates freely chosen as long as ...\" → could be footnotes or description. I'll add as a footnote? Not really footnotes. Maybe add to characteristics type_level as note? The schema doesn't have notes. I'll skip or put in matrix_tables description. I'll add to description field of matrix_tables: \"Flow rates Q1–Q4 can be freely chosen provided Q3 and Q3/Q1 from R49-1 §4.1; Q1/Q2 minimum values with Q2/Q1 = 1.6; Q3/Q4 maximum with Q4/Q3 = 1.25; Q3/Q1 ≥ 40.\"\n\n**Software identification:** Tables 3 & 4. Should I capture? matrix_tables could hold them, or a \"software\" section isn't in schema. I'll add them as matrix_tables entries too (name \"Software identification ...\", rows with software_version, crc_checksum, remark, part_code). Or components characteristics. Simpler: matrix_tables entries.\n\nTable 3 rows: part code groups. I'll encode each row with software_part_code field:\n- 3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality): 03.00.03/B26AFE6B; 03.00.04/6E98963A; 03.02.00/E12E5C04; 03.03.02/D4D1E40B (HMI display bug fixes; low flow output bug fix); 03.03.03/CEEF45F1 (Modbus communication bug fix); 03.03.06/DAC51328; 03.04.00/277BA336\n- 3KXF208402U0313 (Sensus / Logger / Pressure / VKL sensor updater): 02.00.02/84EBD1C8\n- 3KXF208402U0513 (Modbus / Pressure / VKL sensor updater): 02.00.01/C28EAA16\n\nTable 4 (FEW45, footnote 1): groups Measurement (MCU measurement): 1.2.0/94E6FAE9, 1.3.0/A6C913AF; 4G: 1.2.0/AE3D9862, 1.3.0/3B33D2C8, 1.3.1/FDE7CBB6; NB-IoT: 1.3.0/E5742163, 1.3.1/EC4FF9C6, 1.3.2/C8CABC2D. Remark: Software identification for Measurement MCU / Communication MCU. footnote 1.\n\nOK. That's a lot; I'll include them as matrix_tables entries.\n\n**Components:**\n- role \"measuring sensor\" (flow sensor), type_designations: [\"FEW4XY.Z\"] — hmm the flow tube is FEW. Actually the whole meter is FEW4XY.Z? \"An electromagnetic water meter Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\". I believe FEW4 = flow sensor with integral or remote transmitter options (Y indicates integral/remote/sensor-only). FET4* = separate transmitter (calculating and indicating device). Let me define:\n  - component 1: role \"flow sensor\", type_designations [\"AquaMaster4 FEW41Y.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FEW43Y.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"], characteristics: maybe none.\n  - component 2: role \"calculating and indicating device (transmitter)\", type_designations [\"FET4*\"].\n\nAlternatives: null.\n\nHmm, is that accurate? Y = \"1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only)\". So FEW4XY.Z covers integral/remote/sensor-only. FET4* is the transmitter used with remote version. I'll describe components with role \"flow sensor\" (FEW4XY.Z) and role \"calculating and indicating device\" (FET4*). Include module_designation for certified_type: null or \"Not applicable\"? For water meters under R49, the modular approach isn't like R60. I'll set module_designation null. Actually certified_type.category: \"Water meter\".\n\n**certificate.date_issued**: 22 September 2023 → \"2023-09-22\". oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\".\n\n**Number parsing**: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 9\". number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\", revision separate. The schema certificate has \"number\", \"scheme\", \"project_number\", \"page_total\", \"member_state\", \"date_issued\", \"oiml_issuer_id\". Revision isn't in schema but revision_history covers changes; I could include revision in number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 9\"? Better keep number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and note revision 9 in... hmm. Maybe add \"revision\": \"9\"? Not in schema but \"Don't fabricate\" only prohibits inventing content; adding a field present in doc is reasonable. The layered schema is fixed though. I'll include number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 (revision 9)\"? That could break parsing expectations. I'll add a \"revision\" field with \"9\" — extra fields are usually tolerated. Actually rule 8: \"If a field is not present in the markdown, omit it.\" Extra keys aren't prohibited. I'll add \"revision\": \"9\" to certificate.\n\n**Person title**: \"Certification Board\" appears right after date — signature block. person_title: \"Certification Board\"? Hmm, that's the board, not the title of Schmidt. Safer: person_responsible \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\", person_title null (omit). Actually the layout: \"Issuing Authority NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1 / 22 September 2023 / Certification Board\". The name Schmidt is under \"Person responsible\". \"Certification Board\" might be his role/office. I'll omit title to be safe. Hmm, or include \"Certification Board\" as person_title. I'll omit — uncertain.\n\nWait, actually in NMi certificates, the responsible person signs on behalf of the Certification Board. I'll leave person_title out.\n\n**Issuing authority name**: \"NMi Certin B.V.\" with oiml_issuer_id \"NL1\".\n\n**Recommendation accuracy_classes**: [1, 2].\n\n**scheme**: \"A\".\n\nNow, model_family models: I said FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z. But also FET4* transmitter model. Include as model? The family is AquaMaster4. Models: FEW41Y.Z (standard), FEW43Y.Z (advanced), FEW45Y.Z (premium mobile comms). Plus FET4 transmitter. I'll add model entry \"FET4XY.Z\"? The doc says \"FET4*\" — I'll use model_id \"FET4*\" to be faithful.\n\nLet me also double-check Y: \"Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only)\". So Y variants. I'll include in attributes.\n\nNow, model_level entries — the models are FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z. Power supply values differ only in battery count & the included batteries. Also software differs. electromagnetic environment differs for FEW45 (config by antenna). I'll put electromagnetic environment in model_level? It's conditional within FEW45 → config_level better.\n\nmodel_level:\n1. attribute \"ac_mains_supply_voltage\", unit V, values: all three {min 95, max 240}\n2. attribute \"ac_mains_supply_frequency\", unit Hz, all {min 50, max 60}\n3. attribute \"dc_supply_voltage\", unit V, all {min 6, max 32}\n4. attribute \"replaceable_battery_cell_count\", unit null, values: FEW41: 2, FEW43: 2, FEW45: 6\n5. attribute \"replaceable_battery_cell_voltage\", unit V, all 3.6\n6. attribute \"replaceable_battery_operating_voltage_range\", unit V, all {min 2.9, max 3.6}\n7. attribute \"replaceable_batteries_included_with_external_power\", unit null, FEW45: 4 (of 3.6 V each); FEW41/43: 0? The doc for 41/43 doesn't mention batteries with external power → only record FEW45: 4, others null? Values entry with null value is awkward. I'll encode attribute \"batteries_included_with_ac_mains_or_dc_power\" with values: FEW41: \"not applicable\"? Don't fabricate. I'll record only FEW45 with value 4, unit null, and in attributes note \"3.6 V each\". Value: 4, and a sibling attribute \"battery_voltage_of_included_batteries\": 3.6 V. Hmm, getting noisy. I'll do one attribute: \"replaceable_battery_cells_with_external_power_supply\": values [{model FEW45Y.Z, value: \"4 × 3.6 V\"}]? Rule: numbers not strings. \"4x 3.6 V\" — could be two attributes: count 4 and voltage 3.6 V. I'll do:\n- \"backup_battery_count_with_external_power\": FEW45Y.Z: 4 (others omitted)\n- that's it; voltage 3.6 V same as main battery — covered by battery cell voltage attribute (all models 3.6 V).\n\nAlso software identification differs → in matrix_tables.\n\n**Characteristics type_level final list:**\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\" (value string)\n- environmental_class: \"O\"\n- environmental_class_note? \"installed outdoors\" → include as part of value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"? Keep value \"O\", and separate attribute? I'll do value \"O (installed outdoors)\" as string — it's descriptive. Fine.\n- electromagnetic_environment — config-level (see below). Should type_level also have it? Skip to avoid conflict.\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min:-25,max:55}, °C\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\"\n- water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, °C\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6, MPa\n- orientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\"\n- reverse_flow_measured: true (boolean) — value true.\n- checking_facility: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" — covered in recommendation.accuracy_classes; skip in type_level or include. I'll include accuracy_classes only in recommendation.\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: config-level.\n\n**config_level entries:**\n1. electromagnetic_environment:\n   axis: \"transmitter variant and antenna configuration\"\n   values: [{condition: \"FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"E2\"}, {condition: \"FEW45Y.Z transmitter with external antenna cable\", value: \"E1\"}, {condition: \"FEW45Y.Z transmitter without external antenna cable\", value: \"E2\"}]\n2. flow_profile_sensitivity_class:\n   axis: \"bore and meter size\"\n   values: [{condition: \"sizes below DN250\", value: \"U0 and D0 (0 × DN upstream, 0 × DN downstream)\"}, {condition: \"full bore DN250 and larger\", value: \"U3 and D0 (3 × DN upstream, 0 × DN downstream)\"}]\n3. pressure_loss_class:\n   axis: \"bore and meter size\"\n   values: [{condition: \"reduced bore DN40 and DN50 (Δp 40)\", value 0.40, unit bar}, {condition: \"reduced bore larger than DN50 (Δp 63)\", value 0.63, unit bar}, {condition: \"virtual full bore or full bore DN40 and DN50 (Δp 10)\", value 0.10, unit bar}, {condition: \"virtual full bore or full bore DN65 to DN200 (Δp 16)\", value 0.16, unit bar}, {condition: \"virtual full bore or full bore DN250 and larger (Δp 10)\", value 0.10, unit bar}]\n   Value shape: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. The schema example: values[]: {condition, value}. I'll include unit_symbol per rules (structured values strict — every characteristic value must be object with value/unit_symbol). So entries: {\"condition\": ..., \"value\": {\"value\": 0.4, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"}}? Hmm. The structured-value rule says every characteristic value MUST be an object {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. For config_level, values are {condition, value, unit_symbol} per rule 5: \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}.\" So format: {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\", \"value\": 0.4, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"}. OK — flat with condition. Good.\n\nSimilarly model_level values: {model, value, unit_symbol, footnote_markers} — the example in system prompt: {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} with attribute-level unit_symbol. The R137 example puts unit_symbol at attribute level. I'll put unit_symbol at attribute level for model_level (consistent with example), and for config_level per-value unit_symbol since conditions mix units? For pressure loss all in bar → attribute-level unit_symbol \"bar\". I'll follow: config_level entry: {attribute, axis, unit_symbol?, values: [{condition, value}]}. Rule 5 says values {condition, value, unit_symbol} — I'll include unit_symbol inside each value to be safe AND at entry level? Redundant. I'll put unit_symbol at the entry level (like model_level) — matches \"attribute × config-axis matrix\" structure. Hmm, rule 5 explicitly: \"use characteristics.config_level with axis describing the dimension. Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}.\" So I'll put unit_symbol in each value object. Fine.\n\nFor model_level, the R137 example has unit_symbol at attribute level. I'll follow the example.\n\n**matrix_tables:** 8 characteristic tables + 2 software tables = 10 entries. Let me define columns:\n\nFlow tables: columns: [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\"], column_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null]. rows as above. accuracy_class: 1, 2, or \"1 & 2\" → use \"1 & 2\" string or [1,2]? I'll use \"1 & 2\" to be faithful. Hmm, for parseability maybe \"1&2\". Keep \"1 & 2\".\n\nIndicating tables: columns: [\"meter_size\", \"indicating_range_max\", \"indicating_range_min\", \"verification_scale_interval_max\"], units [null, \"m³\", \"m³\", \"m³\"]. meter_size as string list \"DN40, DN50, DN65\".\n\nSoftware tables: columns [\"software_part_code\", \"software_part_description\", \"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\", \"remarks\"], units null.\n\nNames:\n- \"Table 5 — General characteristics of the family of instruments: Reduced bore – AC mains powered\" etc.\n\ndescription field for flow tables: the note about freely chosen flow rates.\n\nfootnotes for table 4 software: [1].\n\n**test_reports dates**: convert to ISO: 2020-08-14, 2020-09-30, 2020-12-24, 2021-08-02, 2021-08-26, 2021-11-23, 2023-09-22.\n\n**revision_history dates**: 2020-08-14 (Initial), 2020-09-30 (1), 2020-12-24 (2), 2021-08-02 (3), 2021-08-26 (4), 2021-11-23 (5), 2022-05-20 (6), 2022-08-30 (7), 2023-07-03 (8), 2023-09-22 (9).\n\n**footnotes:**\n- {\"marker\": \"*\", \"text\": \"With X being 1 for standard or 3 for advanced or 5 for premium (mobile Comms - MC) variant; Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only); Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\"}\n- {\"marker\": \"1)\", \"text\": \"The software version and checksum are displayed on the meter during the circular sequence every 10 minutes. The software version and checksum are displayed sequentially, first for the Measurement part then the Communications part.\"}\n\n**documentation**: test_reports entry {id: \"T11851-7\", role: \"documentation_file\"} — maybe add description? Schema fields: id, date, pages, role. Omit date/pages.\n\nNow, \"project_number\": \"2563125\" (string or number? Keep string \"2563125\"? It's a number; I'll use string to preserve, or int. I'll use \"2563125\" as string.)\n\npage_total: 13.\n\n**category**: \"Water meter\". module_designation: null. description: \"An electromagnetic water meter. Type designations: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*. X = 1 standard, 3 advanced, 5 premium (mobile Comms - MC); Y = 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only); Z = R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\" Maybe keep description short: \"An electromagnetic water meter\".\n\ntype_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"]. Hmm \"FET4*\" — includes marker. I'll write \"AquaMaster4 FET4XY.Z (FET4*)\"? Don't fabricate. Keep \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\". And footnote marker \"*\" applies → type designation is fine.\n\n**components:**\n[\n {role: \"flow sensor\", type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\"], alternatives: null, characteristics: {}},\n {role: \"calculating and indicating device (transmitter)\", type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"], alternatives: null, characteristics: {}}\n]\n\nIs that right though? For integral meters, the transmitter is inside FEW. Hmm. The cert identifies the type as water meter composed of FEW4 (sensor) and FET4 (transmitter). Given revision 9 mentions \"FEW45Y.Z Mobile comms variant of calculating and indicating device\", the transmitter corresponds to X. I'm fairly confident: FEW4 = electromagnetic flow sensor; FET4 = remote transmitter (calculating & indicating device). I'll note in component characteristics: bore options for sensor.\n\nActually let me not over-specify. Components with role and designations, plus characteristics: {\"versions\": ...}? Keep minimal: sensor: characteristics {\"measuring_principle\": \"Electromagnetic\"}? That's at type level. I'll keep components minimal with role + type_designations.\n\nNow the sizeable matrix_tables. Let me carefully write all rows.\n\nTable 5 (Reduced bore, AC mains) rows with accuracy_class values:\n1. {meter_size: \"DN40\", accuracy_class: 1, q1: 0.05, q2: 0.08, q3: 25, q4: 31.25, q3_q1_ratio: 500}\n2. {meter_size: \"DN40\", accuracy_class: 2, q1: 0.025, q2: 0.04, q3: 25, q4: 31.25, q3_q1_ratio: 1000}\n3. {meter_size: \"DN50\", accuracy_class: 1, q1: 0.08, q2: 0.128, q3: 40, q4: 50, q3_q1_ratio: 500}\n4. {meter_size: \"DN50\", accuracy_class: 2, q1: 0.04, q2: 0.064, q3: 40, q4: 50, q3_q1_ratio: 1000}\n5. {meter_size: \"DN65\", accuracy_class: \"1 & 2\", q1: 0.063, q2: 0.1008, q3: 63, q4: 78.75, q3_q1_ratio: 1000}\n6. {meter_size: \"DN80\", accuracy_class: \"1 & 2\", q1: 0.1, q2: 0.16, q3: 100, q4: 125, q3_q1_ratio: 1000}\n7. {DN100, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500}\n8. {DN100, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000}\n9. {DN125, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500}\n10. {DN125, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000}\n11. {DN150, \"1 & 2\": 0.4, 0.64, 400, 500, 1000}\n12. {DN200, \"1 & 2\": 0.63, 1.008, 630, 787.5, 1000}\n13. {DN250, 1: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500}\n14. {DN250, 2: 1, 1.6, 1000, 1250, 1000}\n15. {DN300, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500}\n16. {DN300, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000}\n17. {DN350, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500}\n18. {DN350, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000}\n19. {DN400, \"1 & 2\": 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500}\n20. {DN450, \"1 & 2\": 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500}\n21. {DN500, 1: 8, 12.8, 4000, 5000, 500}\n22. {DN500, 2: 4, 6.4, 4000, 5000, 1000}\n23. {DN600, 1: 12.6, 20.16, 6300, 7875, 500}\n24. {DN600, 2: 6.3, 10.08, 6300, 7875, 1000} — from page 6 continuation; belongs to Table 5.\n\nTable 6 rows as listed earlier (26 rows).\n\nTable 7 rows (44 rows).\n\nTable 8 rows (39 rows).\n\nIndicating tables 9–12 as grouped rows.\n\nSoftware tables 3–4.\n\nTotal JSON is large but OK.\n\nShould I also include model_level q3 etc.? Given matrix_tables capture them, model_level per DN size would duplicate massively. The system prompt example for R137 does both (matrix_tables AND model_level). For R60 it does model_family + model_level without matrix table. Given 4 config dimensions, I'll compromise: model_level only for power-supply attributes (by FEW model) and skip flow-rate model_level (kept in matrix_tables). That respects \"detect matrix\" while staying sane. Actually, let me reconsider: maybe include model_level for q3 (permanent flow rate) per meter size with config_axis note? No — values differ across bore/power/class for same size; a {model, value} list can't hold that without model id encoding config. The matrix_tables are the honest representation. OK.\n\nBut the models list: DN sizes as models? The model family models = FEW41Y.Z/FEW43Y.Z/FEW45Y.Z/FET4*. DN sizes are sizes, not models. Fine.\n\nAlso, matrix_tables description for flow tables should mention the freedom note. I'll add a \"description\" field.\n\nLet me also handle \"Documentation folder no. T11851-7\" → test_reports role documentation_file, id \"T11851-7\".\n\nWait — role list: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. The 7 reports: they are \"results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\" — OIML Type Evaluation Reports. I'll mark all as \"type_evaluation_report\".\n\nNow the Issuing authority address_lines: [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"].\n\nApplicants: [{name: \"ABB Limited\", address_lines: [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]}].\n\nManufacturers: production locations list (4 entries). ABB Limited same address. Include all 4 with addresses from page 13:\n- ABB Limited: [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]\n- ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd.: [\"No. 4528, Kangxin Highway, Pudong New District\", \"Shanghai, 201319\", \"P.R. China\"]\n- ABB Inc.: [\"125 E. County Line Road\", \"Warminster, PA 18974\", \"United States of America\"]\n- ABB India Limited: [\"Plot No. 5 & 6, 2nd Phase, Peenya Industrial Area\", \"Bengaluru – 560058, Karnataka\", \"India\"]\n\nphone: \"+31 88 636 2332\". email: \"certin@nmi.nl\". website: \"www.nmi.nl\".\n\ncertificate.date_issued: \"2023-09-22\".\n\nLet me also decide value formats: q values numeric, decimals converted: 0,05 → 0.05 etc. 787,5 → 787.5. 100,8 → 100.8. 253,9 → 253.9. 406,2 → 406.2. 0,0625 → 0.0625. 0,39375 → 0.39375. 0,1575 → 0.1575. 15,625 → 15.625. 157,5 → 157.5. 39,375 → 39.375. 317,5 → 317.5. 507,9 → 507.9. 12,8→12.8. 20,16→20.16. 6,3→6.3. 10,08→10.08. 2,56→2.56. 5,12→5.12. 0,1008→0.1008. 0,2016→0.2016. 0,8064→0.8064. 0,4032→0.4032. 4,032→4.032. 8,064→8.064. 20,48→20.48. 10,24→10.24. 40,32→40.32. 25,6→25.6. 25,2→25.2. 25,4? no. 254 stays. 406,3→406.3. 1,024→1.024. 0,128→0.128. 0,064→0.064. 0,512→0.512. 0,256→0.256. 0,64→0.64. 1,6→1.6. 3,2→3.2. 6,4→6.4. 2,016→2.016. 1,008→1.008. 0,32→0.32. 0,16→0.16. 0,25→0.25. 0,4→0.4. 0,1→0.1. 0,025? none here. 0,08→0.08. 0,04→0.04. 0,05→0.05. 0,63→0.63. 0,252→0.252.\n\nIndicating: 0,0001→0.0001; 0,001→0.001; 0,01→0.01; 0,1→0.1; 1→1. 9999999/99999999/999999999 ints.\n\nNow assemble JSON. Size is big; be careful with valid JSON.\n\nAlso add certificate fields: number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\", revision \"9\", scheme \"A\", project_number \"2563125\", page_total 13, member_state \"The Netherlands\", date_issued \"2023-09-22\", oiml_issuer_id \"NL1\".\n\nrecommendation: {id: \"R49-1\", edition: 2013, scheme: \"A\", accuracy_classes: [1, 2], title maybe}. Schema fields: id, edition, amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes. No title field. I could add \"title\" — extra. I'll add title as extra? Keep minimal; skip title.\n\nHmm edition int 2013 — R49-1:2013 edition year. Good.\n\nFootnote marker for the type designation asterisk: the type_designations \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\" — the * refers to the footnote. In certified_type I could add footnote_markers? Not in schema for type_designations. Fine, footnotes array holds it.\n\nAlso certificate number includes \"revision 9\" — I'll keep number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and add \"revision\": \"9\".\n\nLet me now also think about \"Water temperature class T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C)\": T50 class per R49 means water temp up to 50 °C. Range +0.1 to +50 °C. Encode:\n- water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n- water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n\ntemperature_range_ambient: {value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}.\n\nmaximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"} and add separate \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\": {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}? The doc: \"1,6 MPa (16 bar)\". I'll record one with value 1.6 MPa and include the bar in a second entry — or value {value: 1.6, unit MPa} plus footnote-ish. I'll add two attributes: maximum_admissible_pressure (MPa) and maximum_admissible_pressure (bar). Use keys: maximum_admissible_pressure and maximum_admissible_pressure_bar? Slightly redundant but clear. Actually cleaner: one attribute maximum_admissible_pressure with unit MPa value 1.6, and the parenthetical converted: skip bar. But 16 bar is useful. I'll include both attributes.\n\nmechanical_class \"M1\", environmental_class \"O (installed outdoors)\".\n\norientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\".\n\nreverse_flow: value true. Attribute \"reverse_flow_measured\": true.\n\nchecking_facility: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\". Both tables list it (Table 1 has it on page 3 top; Table 2 has it as row). Good — type_level.\n\nAccuracy class 1 and 2 — include type_level accuracy_class? recommendation covers it. Skip.\n\nSoftware identification: \"See details in table 3/4\" — skip type_level; software tables in matrix_tables.\n\nNow write model_level:\n\n```json\n\"model_level\": [\n  {\"attribute\": \"ac_mains_supply_voltage\", \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"values\": [\n    {\"model\": \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"value\": {\"min\": 95, \"max\": 240}},\n    {\"model\": \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"value\": {\"min\": 95, \"max\": 240}},\n    {\"model\": \"FEW45Y.Z\", \"value\": {\"min\": 95, \"max\": 240}}]},\n  {\"attribute\": \"ac_mains_supply_frequency\", \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\", \"values\": [all {min:50,max:60}]},\n  {\"attribute\": \"dc_powered_supply_voltage\", \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"values\": [all {min:6, max:32}]},\n  {\"attribute\": \"replaceable_battery_cell_count\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [\n    {\"model\": \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"value\": 2}, {\"model\": \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"value\": 2}, {\"model\": \"FEW45Y.Z\", \"value\": 6}]},\n  {\"attribute\": \"replaceable_battery_cell_voltage\", \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"values\": [all 3.6]},\n  {\"attribute\": \"replaceable_battery_operating_voltage_range\", \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"values\": [all {min:2.9, max:3.6}]},\n  {\"attribute\": \"replaceable_battery_cells_included_with_mains_or_dc_power\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [\n    {\"model\": \"FEW45Y.Z\", \"value\": 4}]}\n]\n```\n\nHmm for the last: FEW45 AC mains & DC include 4× 3.6 V replaceable batteries. FEW41/43: not stated. Only one value → maybe better as type_level? It's model-specific; keep with single value.\n\nWait, does FEW45's 6× vs 4× make sense? 6 replaceable battery cells total, 4 included when externally powered? That's what the doc implies. OK, record as-is: attribute \"replaceable_battery_cells_with_external_power_supply\" value 4.\n\nconfig_level as planned.\n\nNow matrix_tables. Let me write them all out.\n\nTable 5:\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Table 5 – General characteristics of the family of instruments: Reduced bore – AC mains powered\",\n  \"columns\": [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null],\n  \"rows\": [...24 rows...],\n  \"description\": \"Flow rates Q1–Q4 can be freely chosen provided: Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 are selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013; Q1 and Q2 are minimum values and Q2/Q1 = 1.6; Q3 and Q4 are maximum values and Q4/Q3 = 1.25; the ratio Q3/Q1 is at least 40.\",\n  \"footnotes\": []\n}\n```\n\nSame description for tables 6, 7, 8.\n\nIndicating tables: name includes table number and config.\n\nSoftware Table 3:\ncolumns: [\"software_part_code\", \"software_part_description\", \"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\", \"remarks\"]\nrows:\n- {code \"3KXF208402U0113\", desc \"Full functionality\", \"03.00.03\", \"B26AFE6B\", null}\n- ... etc\n- {\"3KXF208402U0313\", \"Sensus / Logger / Pressure / VKL sensor updater\", \"02.00.02\", \"84EBD1C8\", null}\n- {\"3KXF208402U0513\", \"Modbus / Pressure / VKL sensor updater\", \"02.00.01\", \"C28EAA16\", null}\n\nTable 4:\ncolumns: [\"software_function\", \"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\", \"remarks\"]\nrows:\n- {Measurement, 1.2.0, 94E6FAE9, \"Software identification for Measurement MCU\"}\n- {Measurement, 1.3.0, A6C913AF, same}\n- {4G, 1.2.0, AE3D9862, \"Software identification for Communication MCU\"}\n- {4G, 1.3.0, 3B33D2C8, same}\n- {4G, 1.3.1, FDE7CBB6, same}\n- {NB-IoT, 1.3.0, E5742163, same}\n- {NB-IoT, 1.3.1, EC4FF9C6, same}\n- {NB-IoT, 1.3.2, C8CABC2D, same}\nfootnotes: [\"1)\"]\n\nNote the model: FEW45Y.Z variant.\n\nShould the two software tables be matrix_tables or under a component? matrix_tables is fine.\n\nGiven output size constraints, this will be a long JSON but let's do it.\n\nAlso add \"certificate\" page count etc.\n\nOne more check — applicant table: \"Applicant and Manufacturer: ABB Limited ...\". So applicant = manufacturer = ABB Limited. manufacturers[] should list production locations (4). Applicants: [ABB Limited].\n\nLet me now also reconsider whether to include \"FET4*\" as separate certified model: The \"Identification of the certified type\" is the water meter comprising FEW4XY.Z and FET4*. In model_family models, I'll include 3 FEW variants + note FET4 transmitter. I'll add a 4th model entry: {model_id: \"FET4*\", label: \"Calculating and indicating device (flow transmitter)\"}? Hmm, \"FET4*\" might actually be FET400 transmitter... The asterisk footnote gives X/Y/Z pattern for it too. I'll include model \"FET4XY.Z\"? Don't fabricate — the doc says \"FET4*\". Use \"FET4*\".\n\nHmm wait, actually re-reading: \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4<sup>*</sup>\" — this means types FEW4XY.Z and FET4XY.Z? The * footnote then explains X, Y, Z. Since the footnote defines X, Y, Z patterns, and FET4* presumably is FET4XY.Z. But to be faithful: type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4XY.Z\"]? I'll go with what's printed: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\" and \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\". Good enough; the footnote clarifies the naming scheme.\n\nModels list:\n- {\"model_id\": \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"label\": \"Standard variant (X = 1)\", \"attributes\": {\"bore_options\": \"Z = R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore), F (full bore)\", \"version_options\": \"Y = 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only)\"}}\n- {\"model_id\": \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"label\": \"Advanced variant (X = 3)\", \"attributes\": same}\n- {\"model_id\": \"FEW45Y.Z\", \"label\": \"Premium variant with mobile comms (X = 5)\", \"attributes\": same}\n- {\"model_id\": \"FET4*\", \"label\": \"Calculating and indicating device (transmitter)\", \"attributes\": {}}\n\nfamily_name: \"AquaMaster4\".\n\nNow config_level:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"electromagnetic_environment\",\n    \"axis\": \"model and antenna cable configuration\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z\", \"value\": \"E2\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"FEW45Y.Z transmitter with external antenna cable\", \"value\": \"E1\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"FEW45Y.Z transmitter without external antenna cable\", \"value\": \"E2\"}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\",\n    \"axis\": \"bore and meter size\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"meter sizes below DN250\", \"value\": \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore sizes DN250 and larger\", \"value\": \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"pressure_loss\",\n    \"axis\": \"bore and meter size\",\n    \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50 (pressure loss class Δp 40)\", \"value\": 0.4},\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, sizes larger than DN50 (pressure loss class Δp 63)\", \"value\": 0.63},\n      {\"condition\": \"virtual full bore or full bore, DN40 and DN50 (pressure loss class Δp 10)\", \"value\": 0.1},\n      {\"condition\": \"virtual full bore or full bore, DN65 to DN200 (pressure loss class Δp 16)\", \"value\": 0.16},\n      {\"condition\": \"virtual full bore or full bore, DN250 and larger (pressure loss class Δp 10)\", \"value\": 0.1}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"power_supply_voltage\",\n    \"axis\": \"power source\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"AC mains\", \"value\": {\"min\": 95, \"max\": 240}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"DC powered (renewable energy)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 6, \"max\": 32}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"replaceable battery (cell voltage)\", \"value\": 3.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nHmm rule 5: values {condition, value, unit_symbol} — include unit_symbol per value. OK did that for power_supply_voltage; do same for pressure_loss (unit in each) and keep \"unit_symbol\" out of entry level to avoid duplication? I'll put unit_symbol inside each value object for config_level consistently, no entry-level unit.\n\nAlso note \"Δp\" contains a special char — fine in JSON.\n\nAvoid \"×\"? Use \"x\". Fine.\n\nNow temperature: also record \"temperature_range_ambient\" applies \"for both flow sensor and flow transmitter\" — could add note in value? Keep simple.\n\nNow — one consideration: page 3 table \"Checking facilities: Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\" is continuation of Table 1. Good.\n\nWrite the final JSON. Also include footnotes with markers \"*\" and \"1)\